From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 01:43:46 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] rpcrt4: size a union that is an argument with a pointer arm
 (3213)

NdrNonEncapsulatedUnion/NdrEncapsulatedUnion sizing of a pointer arm assumed the union sits inside
a structure whose pointer layout pass had set PointerLength.  For a union that is itself a
parameter ([in, switch_is(x)] union *, as RControlServiceEx[AW] declares in MS-SCMR) there is no such
pass: PointerLength was 0, the code logged "BufferLength == 0??" and sized the referent at offset 0,
so the client overflowed its buffer.  The referent now follows the pointer id, as the marshaller
already writes it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XZFS4SPPzeH5d2c1MuzqCS

diff --git a/dlls/rpcrt4/ndr_marshall.c b/dlls/rpcrt4/ndr_marshall.c
index c68eb0e..87934eb 100644
--- a/dlls/rpcrt4/ndr_marshall.c
+++ b/dlls/rpcrt4/ndr_marshall.c
@@ -5897,10 +5897,19 @@ static void union_arm_buffer_size(PMIDL_STUB_MESSAGE pStubMsg,
                 if (!pStubMsg->IgnoreEmbeddedPointers)
                 {
                     int saved_buffer_length = pStubMsg->BufferLength;
+#ifdef SG_MUTANT_RPC_UNIONPTR
+                    if (0)
+#else
+                    if (!pStubMsg->PointerLength)
+#endif
+                    {
+                        /* a union that is not inside a structure (an argument): there are no
+                         * deferred pointers, the referent follows the pointer id */
+                        PointerBufferSize(pStubMsg, *(unsigned char **)pMemory, desc);
+                        break;
+                    }
                     pStubMsg->BufferLength = pStubMsg->PointerLength;
                     pStubMsg->PointerLength = 0;
-                    if(!pStubMsg->BufferLength)
-                        ERR("BufferLength == 0??\n");
                     PointerBufferSize(pStubMsg, *(unsigned char **)pMemory, desc);
                     pStubMsg->PointerLength = pStubMsg->BufferLength;
                     pStubMsg->BufferLength = saved_buffer_length;
